      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-183541",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following sequence that converges to $L = 2$:\n\n$a_{n} = \\frac{2n^{2}}{n^{2} - 10}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $\u03b5 > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(\u03b5)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid < \u03b5$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, simplifying $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$2n^{2}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nNotice that\n{{field-3}}\nfor any natural\n{{field-4}}.\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$n^{2}$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid < \u03b5$ for $n$:\n\n$n^{2} - 10$ {{field-7}} {{field-8}} {{field-9}} {{field-10}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$\u03b5$ {{field-11}} {{field-12}}\n\nTherefore, $\\mid \\frac{5^{n + 1}}{5^{n} + 2} - 5 \\mid$\n{{field-13}}\nfor all natural numbers\n{{field-14}}."
